--- machine\system\network\resolver
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Resolver

resolvd's configuration. Everything here is a fallback or an override:
the live facts — which interface has which servers and search domains,
which carries the default route — come from netd over its control socket
and are never written to the registry. resolvd reads this key whole on
start and on every change.

Default security: inherits the Machine hive root (SYSTEM and Administrators
full, Authenticated Users read).

--- machine\system\network\resolver servers
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Resolver Servers
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (none)
applies: live

Servers to use when no interface supplies any — a machine with a static
address and no DNS in its profile, or nothing but a link-local address.
Never consulted while any interface contributes servers; this is not a
"public resolver as well" setting.

--- machine\system\network\resolver searchdomains
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Resolver SearchDomains
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (none)
applies: live

Search domains applied to single-label names after every interface's own,
in order. A single-label name with no applicable domain is `NotFound`
without a query; it is never sent bare upstream.

--- machine\system\network\resolver controlsecurity
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Resolver ControlSecurity
type: REG_BINARY
default: Everyone: RESOLVER_QUERY; SYSTEM, Administrators: all
applies: live

The resolvd control object's descriptor, self-relative. Checked against
the peer's token on every request to `/run/resolvd/resolv.sock`. Rights:
`RESOLVER_QUERY` (0x1) for `resolve`, `lookup`, `reverse` and `status`;
`RESOLVER_CONTROL` (0x2) for `flush`. Absent or malformed, the compiled
default above applies. The stub listener on 127.0.0.53 is not governed by
this object: DNS carries no caller identity, so that door is anonymous
and everything on the machine may ask.

--- machine\system\network\resolver\hosts
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Resolver\Hosts

Static names: the value name is the hostname, the data an address
(REG_SZ) or several (REG_MULTI_SZ). An exact, case-insensitive match
wins over DNS and is answered at every door — the native socket, the NSS
shim, and the stub listener alike, so a Go binary that speaks DNS itself
sees the same answer. Reverse lookups of these addresses return the name.
This is what `/etc/hosts` was; there is no `/etc/hosts` on Peios.

--- machine\system\network\resolver\policy
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Resolver\Policy

Reserved for transport and validation policy — `DNSSEC`, `DNSOverTLS`,
`mDNS` — none of which resolvd v1 implements (PEI-501). Values here are
read and ignored, so an image may set its intent ahead of the
implementation. `.local` is never forwarded to DNS (RFC 6762) and LLMNR is
never spoken.
